From: chris@efi.com (Chris Phoenix)
Date: Wed, 30 Mar 1994 18:04:50 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-3-376] interspecies conflict

> Reilly Jones saith:
> > I am troubled by the potential conflict of rights between
> > post-humans and humans (or Sapient AI) if members of the
> > two groups develop mutually exclusive purposes.
> 
> If rights can conflict (some might say it's impossible),
> why should the scope of such conflict be any different
> because more than one kind of person is concerned?
> An example might help.

I haven't heard a lot about animal rights on this group, so I'm not
sure if there's a general extropian consensus.  I don't have a strong
opinion.  But your question made me wonder whether we should look to
the way we deal with animals for analogies.  Here are four separate
thoughts.  I don't have time to think them through carefully, but I
hope they will be more interesting than annoying.

  For example, it seems to me that painlessly instantly killing an
animal without warning is not a bad thing to do.  It doesn't cause
suffering, and it doesn't increase entropy very much.  On the other
hand, a future post-human (individual or society) could apply this
reasoning to gradually breed us into docility or whatever--no
individual humans would be hurt, we'd be happier not fighting each
other, and they certainly don't need our brains for anything!  All it
requires is for them to devalue our humanness (extropians do this
already) or our society (almost everyone does this) or our
intelligence (we think it's pretty good, but they won't).  (This is
the type of reasoning used by AIs in several science fiction stories
where they're supposed to help humans but end up stifling them or
controlling them utterly.)

  Obviously, most of us don't think that every organism has a right
not to be killed.  But we think that humans have a right not to be
killed, or interfered with in general.  We also think humans have a
right to eat.  If animals or plants wanted a right not to be killed,
these rights would conflict.  Or am I misusing the term "right"?

  There may be rights that apply to species rather than individuals.
For example, the right to evolve or not to evolve.  This is a right
that no one thinks about (except that our right to change may be being
taken away by people opposing genetic experiments on humans, and some
say that our evolution is being slowed by our efforts to keep
somewhat-damaged humans alive).  But as alluded to in my first
example, it may be possible, with nothing more than clever
advertising, for a post-human to control our evolution.  (Look at
standards of attractiveness for women now!  In a few generations,
women might all weigh under 100 pounds!)  

  What about the right not to have one's environment change
drastically and uncontrollably?  I've often wondered what goes on in
my cat's brain when I turn the lights on and off.  He doesn't *seem*
to mind...  What about the right not to be uploaded?  At some point,
it may be easier for the post-humans to keep us around by uploading us
and simulating our environment.  They might move between hardware as
easily as we walk, and they might debate about whether it would
infringe on our rights if we didn't know the difference.  They might
never bother to ask us, any more than I've measured the adrenaline
level in my cat's bloodstream while I turn the light off.

