Subject: [#94-3-377] Criticism

Reilly,

I've read everything you've written since you joined the extropians
list and I thought you would like some constructive criticism.

The overarching problem I have with your posts is that they're 
usually written in an almost unreadable style.  When trying to 
communicate with others, I aim to be clear-- I want to minimize 
the amount of misunderstanding caused by my own words so I can
get at the ideas themselves.  However, your posts are invariably
dense with verbiage.  Your use of either "objective reality," 
"subjective reality," or "consensual reality" in every other
sentence wears thin after the first few posts. 

Also, I'll mention that the application of a label is not an argument,
neither is mentioning a string of philosophers you don't like.  Calling
something "The French Disease" is not a refutation, especially if you
have never posted a refutation of said disease in the first place.
I'd much rather see you put forth the arguments than simply affix
a label.

I've included the latest post I read, I'll make some general comments
that are just as applicable to most of your other posts. 


  Eric Forst wrote 3/25/94: <If we find that two beliefs of which we are 
  certain lead to a logical contradiction, we reject the belief of which
  we are less certain.

  This continuous, sloppy mixing of terminology is wearing thin.  You are 
  certain of knowledge, facts, truth.  You are uncertain of beliefs.  
  Beliefs are relative.  Certainty is absolute, "free from doubt."  There 
  is simply no such thing as "less certain."  You are either certain or 
  you are uncertain.  You can be more uncertain or less uncertain.  Mere
  repetition of your position of "we reject the belief of which we are less
  certain" over and over and over does not increase it's intelligibility, 
  it merely indicates fuzzy, wooden-headed, relativistic argumentation.
  
Here we have two people using one word differently.  Your definition
of "certainty" differs from Eric's, otherwise Eric would be have the
same problems as you have with his usage.  Nonetheless, you proceed
with your definition of "certain" and it's as if you never tried to
understand what was meant by it.  Maybe "certain" was a fitting word
for the idea, maybe you could have suggested a better one, but you
didn't give the idea much thought here, only the label.

Notice your "....relativistic argumentation" at the end, why not
put forth the arguments against "relativistice argumentation"
(whatever that is, and whatever they are) instead of just naming
it?  I don't know what you mean by the label, and I can't very well
judge the arguments (or their applicability) if you never present 
them.
  

  Eric again: <In MATTER AND CONSCIOUSNESS, Paul Churchland discusses the
  fallibility of our access to our internal mental states. "Subjective" 
  reality is observed fallibly and imperfectly, just the same way that 
  objective reality is observed. Experimental evidence for this is well 
  established, and Churchland provides some good references to the literature.
  My observation of and reporting on my present "subjective" mental state is
  neither more nor less fallible than my observation of and reporting on the
  objects on my desk at this moment.>
 
  Churchland's brand of functionalism and his arguments for the fallibility of
  knowing your own thoughts are rejected by me in favor of the biological
  epistemology of Edelman as modified by Kauffman's spontaneous order theory
  applied to the neuronal somatic selection system and Searle's "no deep
  unconscious" models.  It is silly to argue that I don't know what I am 
  thinking in subjective reality.  Knower-and-known systems such as this are 
  whole, not fragmented as Churchland posits.  Fallibility within consensual
  reality is commonplace, that is, when you attempt to communicate your mental
  states and thoughts to others.
  
It's as if you look for any opportunity to say "X reality."  Compare:

(1) It is silly to argue that I don't know what I am thinking in subjective
   reality.

(2) It is silly to argue that I don't know what I am thinking.

Your phrase "subjective reality" adds nothing to the sentence.  What 
I am thinking is, obviously, subjective.

Compare:

(1) Fallibility within consensual reality is commonplace, that is, when 
    you attempt to communicate your mental states and thoughts to others.

(2) Fallibility is commonplace when you attempt to communicate your mental
    states and thoughts to others.

Well, I could go on, but I hope you see my point.  The more clear and 
concise your writing, the more people will bother to read it.  You 
use "X reality" so much it annoys.  To label is not to argue.

