While we're at it:

1) They believe in the exploitation theory of property rights, not the
protection theory we do.

2) They blame the market for failures caused by the State.

3) They have little to no grasp of economics, such as the theory of value,
money, credit, banking, business cycles, price theory - especially the
theory that prices signal shortages and surpluses of goods and what their
best allocation is - etc.

4) They assume that land is originally owned by everyone, rather than by
no one.

5) They have Rousseau's uncritical admiration for primitive societies as
long as they aren't the ones Western civilization came from.  If they are
ancient Greece, Rome, Europe, etc., then their faults are emphasized to
the exclusion of their virtues with no allowance made for the stage of
development they were in at the time they exhibited the fault, whether
it was gotten rid of, or whether other societies had similar faults in
greater degree.

That's all I can think of for now, but I'm sure they'll remind me of more
of them.
